<!--
  Salah ships a deliberately **partial** Bangla translation
  (`app/src/main/res/values-bn/strings.xml`), and this file is where that decision is recorded.

  The strings the Bangla file defines are the ones whose meaning is unambiguous: the names of the
  calendars, months, prayers and observances, the short navigation and calendar labels, and the language
  selector's own labels. The explanatory strings — the ones that state what is an estimate, what Salah
  cannot verify, and which authority it does not claim to match — stay in English until they can be
  reviewed by someone competent to review that wording. Translating them loosely would change an
  accuracy claim, which is exactly the kind of change this app must not make silently.

  The **Calendar** screen is the one screen this policy does not allow to be partial: every string it
  can show is translated. Its completeness is enforced by `LocalizationResourcesTest` rather than by
  lint, because this ignore would otherwise stay quiet about a forgotten calendar key too. The
  interface language is a stored preference with its own selector on Settings > General — a choice the
  app applies at runtime, not a build-time decision; see docs/LOCALIZATION.md.

  Android's resource resolution falls back to `values/` for anything a locale does not define, so the
  app is complete in either case; this is a supported configuration rather than a defect.
  `MissingTranslation` is a *translation policy* check rather than a correctness one, so this is the
  answer Salah gives it.

  The ignore is scoped to Bangla by message, so adding any other locale without translating it is
  still an error. Every other lint check keeps the severity configured in `app/build.gradle.kts`
  (`abortOnError = true`, `warningsAsErrors = false`).
-->
<lint>
    <issue id="MissingTranslation">
        <ignore regexp="is not translated in &quot;bn&quot;" />
    </issue>
</lint>
